You Google your own business. You know it's good. Customers love it. But when you type in the exact kind of search a new customer would use, like "plumber near me" or "best HVAC repair in [your city]," your business is nowhere on the first page. Meanwhile, a competitor with a worse website, maybe even worse reviews, shows up right at the top.

It's frustrating, and it's more common than you'd think. Here's what's actually going on, and what fixes it.

## "Near Me" searches work differently than you'd expect

When someone searches "near me," Google isn't just looking at your website. It's pulling from a separate system called the local pack, the map and three listings that show up at the top of search results. That local pack is driven almost entirely by your Google Business Profile, not your website.

If you've never claimed and set up your Google Business Profile, or if it's incomplete, outdated, or inconsistent with how your business is listed elsewhere online, you're effectively invisible for the exact searches that matter most. A beautiful website with zero Google Business Profile work behind it will lose to a plain website with a fully optimized profile, almost every time.

## Three things usually explain a missing listing

**Your profile isn't fully set up.** Google rewards complete, active profiles. That means accurate categories, service areas, hours, photos, and regular updates, not a profile you claimed once and never touched again.

**Your business information isn't consistent across the web.** Google cross-checks your name, address, and phone number against directories like Yelp, Apple Maps, Bing Places, and others. If your address is formatted differently on three different sites, or an old phone number is still floating around on a directory you forgot existed, it creates doubt that search engines resolve by ranking you lower.

**You don't have enough recent reviews, or any at all.** Review count, review recency, and your response to reviews all factor into local ranking. A business with 60 reviews from two years ago can rank below a newer business with 15 reviews from last month.

## Why this gets worse over time, not better

None of this fixes itself. Directory listings drift out of sync as you move locations or change numbers. Reviews slow down without a system prompting customers to leave them. Competitors who are actively managing their presence pull further ahead the longer you're not.

The businesses that consistently show up in that local pack usually aren't the ones with the biggest budget. They're the ones treating their online presence as something that needs regular, ongoing attention, the same way they'd maintain a storefront or a service vehicle.

## What actually fixes it

Getting found for "near me" searches comes down to three ongoing things working together: a fully built-out and actively maintained Google Business Profile, consistent business information across every directory that matters, and a steady flow of recent reviews. None of these are one-time projects. They're maintenance, the same way your equipment needs regular service to keep running well.

If you've checked your own listing and didn't love what you saw, that's usually the first sign it's worth having someone look at the whole picture, not just the website.